-- PERMANENT negative control (PLAN_ELABORATOR_ERGONOMICS.md step E2), and
-- since 2026-07-26 the load-bearing half of the pair.
--
-- Expected-carrier propagation lifts a Natural variable through unary `-`
-- only when the surrounding expression supplies a registered upper carrier.
-- With no expected carrier, `-m` must remain rejected rather than guessing
-- Integer (or any other carrier).
--
-- A negated numeral LITERAL is now different: `-1` lands in Integer, because
-- a literal has no intrinsic type to drift away from. A variable does — `m`
-- is a Natural — so reading `-m` as an integer would turn an error at the
-- site into a confusing failure downstream, which is exactly what the
-- ℕ-narrowing discipline is trying to avoid. The distinction is enforced in
-- the unary dispatch and holds with the whole ℕ→ℤ→ℚ→ℝ tower in scope, not
-- just in this file's narrow import set.
module ErrorTest.unseeded_negative_natural

import Natural.basics
import Natural.arithmetic
import Integer.basics
import Real.interface

definition ErrorTest.control (m : ℕ) : Proposition := -m = -m
